{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793044908,"nonce":"LsY1tADRoILq5hcdHvg/fOkp6tI=","title":"Keep macro asset indexes valid after identical generated-file rewrites","message":"CSS macros repeatedly invoke the external compiler and read the same generated binary output. File.getBytes/getContent previously captured only metadata stamps. An identical rewrite changes mtime, making the macro dependency stale even though its contents are unchanged; subsequent hover requests can schedule another full analysis.\n\nThe unchanged NewProject.hx debug probe reached ready after 540.601 seconds, then its first hover timed out after another 90 seconds. The focused real dialog markup works with a larger debug macro budget, including custom RayDE CSS classes. Identical generated-file rewrites are a concrete cache-invalidating path; verify the full-page stall with the optimized release before attributing all of that delay to this path.\n\nRecord a digest of the first bytes actually read by each macro data-file dependency. Keep the cheap metadata comparison for unchanged files; if only its timestamp changed, compare content. Different content must still invalidate, and later reads must not replace the first-read digest. Source parser dependencies retain their strict source stamps. Advance the persisted editor payload schema for the dependency representation.\n\nAdd an end-to-end session regression that opens a page using a macro-read asset, rewrites identical bytes with a changed mtime, and confirms a hover without a new checking cycle; also assert different bytes invalidate the original dependency snapshot. Related cache issue: 5133d1c60ca8482260274f1567b31f76ca60e644d5769cc00fcc01df709fee9d.","files":null}]}